page cmd_up {
  title = "vmlab up"
  chapter_header "vmlab up" {
    kicker       = "Commands · reference"
    reading_time = "5 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-30"
    version      = "vmlab 0.35"
  }

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab up` brings the [lab](guide_lab_file) in the current directory to
    life: it validates the lab file, starts the lab daemon, downloads any
    registry [template](guide_templates) or image that is not in the store yet,
    creates each machine's clone, boots the machines in dependency order and
    runs their provision scripts and playbooks. With machine names it does the
    same for a subset of the lab.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab up [--record <VM=PATH>]... [VMS]..." }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`[VMS]...`" | "Machines to bring up. With none given, every VM and container in the lab." |
      | "`--record <VM=PATH>`" | "Record that VM's console to `PATH` from the first frame of its boot; repeatable, one VM each. The recording outlives the verb. See [vmlab record](cmd_record)." |
      | "`--fps`, `--size`, `--compress-idle[=SECS]`, `--timeline`, `--max-duration`" | "Recording options, applied to every `--record`." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  h2 "What it does"

  p <<DOC
    The verb runs a full [validation](cmd_validate) of the lab file before it
    has any side effect. A lab that does not validate is never started, and the
    issues are printed with the offending text underlined in the source.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    It then connects to the supervisor, starting it if none is running, and
    asks it for the lab daemon. The lab daemon owns everything that follows and
    outlives this process: the workspace syncer, the network fabric and the
    machines themselves keep running after `vmlab up` returns. See
    [How vmlab runs a lab](guide_architecture) for the daemon layout.
  DOC

  p "Inside the daemon, `up` works through a plan computed before anything is touched:"

  list {
    style = :numbered
    li "Machines the subset names through `depends_on` or `depends_on_provision` are added, and `up` prints each one it pulls in. The machines are grouped into waves by `depends_on`: a machine's wave starts once every machine it names is ready. Readiness is all it waits for, never their provisioning. Anything the plan skips is printed with its reason."
    li "Deferred downloads run first, with progress streamed to the terminal. This is the same code path [vmlab pull](cmd_pull) runs on its own."
    li "The host's QEMU, firmware and helper binaries are checked for every target machine."
    li "The bundled SMB server starts when the lab declares [shared folders](guide_shares), so shares are reachable during provisioning."
    li "Each wave boots in parallel. A machine carrying a first-boot script runs it before it counts as ready. If one machine in a wave fails, the rest of the wave is aborted and the verb fails; the machine that failed is left running for inspection."
    li "For a VM that `up` waits on anyway, because it has a first-boot script, a provision or playbook, or a dependent, `up` refreshes the agent if it is out of date once it answers: after the first-boot script and before any provision (see below)."
    li "Each machine runs its own provision scripts and playbooks in declaration order once it is ready and its [share mounts](guide_shares) have finished. Machines provision in parallel with each other and with later waves still booting. A machine that declares `depends_on_provision` holds its first step until every machine it names has finished all of its steps."
    li "Port forwards are installed, and the workspace syncer starts for every dev machine that declares a workspace (see [Dev machines and the workspace syncer](guide_dev))."
    li "The verb waits, for at most 30 seconds, for the [share mounts](guide_shares) of every VM no provision or playbook waited on to finish, and prints a `WARNING:` line for each share that will not mount or is still failing when the wait runs out."
  }

  p <<DOC
    Output from provision scripts and playbooks streams to the terminal as it
    happens, each line prefixed with the name of the machine whose step wrote
    it, because several machines' steps run at once. The first step that
    fails fails `up` and cancels the steps still running on other machines;
    `up` returns only once every step has finished or been cancelled. On
    success the last line is `lab "<name>" is up`. A VM declared with
    `gui = true` (or a lab that sets it) gets a detached VNC viewer opened from
    this terminal, since the daemon is headless; closing the viewer only
    disconnects, the VM keeps running.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    Shared folders are mounted once each VM is ready, after its first-boot
    script, and a mount step may retry for five minutes. A VM's provisions
    and playbooks wait for its mounts to finish, and `up` prints its mount
    report before the first of them runs. So that a share that cannot mount
    is not left for you to find by using it, `up` also waits for the mounts
    of every other VM before its last line. The wait ends as soon as every mount
    task has finished, and at most 30 seconds after the rest of `up` is done.
    A VM with shares that nothing else waits on therefore holds `up` until
    its guest answers and its shares are mounted, or for those 30 seconds.
    Each share that will not mount gets a line naming the VM, the share and
    the reason:
  DOC

  code text {
    source = <<TXT
WARNING: "lin01": share "data" will not mount: `mount` refused: exited 64: ...
WARNING: "lin01": share "blocked" not mounted yet, still retrying (attempt 1 of 30): exited 1: mkdir: can't create directory '/etc/hostname/': Not a directory
TXT
  }

  p <<DOC
    A share still retrying when the wait runs out keeps retrying, and its
    `share.unmountable` event reaches [vmlab logs](cmd_logs) if it gives up.
    On Windows, a share whose `net use` still fails with system error 67 gets
    a plain line instead of a warning: the SMB client takes a few minutes
    after boot to start, so the share is expected to mount moments later.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    `up` is idempotent for a machine that is already running: the daemon leaves
    it alone. Clones persist across [vmlab down](cmd_down) and are only removed
    by [vmlab destroy](cmd_destroy), so a second `up` after a `down` boots the
    same disks and skips the first-boot script.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    In a linked git worktree whose main checkout is frozen, `up` builds each
    machine that has no disk yet as a clone of the freeze rather than of its
    template, when the freeze holds it under the same `template` or `image`
    reference. It boots as if the freeze point had just been restored, and
    every lab of the family runs under worktree isolation. See
    [Worktree labs](guide_worktrees).
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    In a frozen family each lab's fixed host ports are shifted by its port
    slot, and `up` prints one line for each forward it installs somewhere
    other than its declared port:
  DOC

  code text { source = <<'SRC'
"web": container port declared on host port 18080 is bound on 18081 (worktree port slot, PRD §21.7)
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    A forward installed after `up` returns, once its machine takes a lease,
    is not printed. The lab daemon writes the same line to its log, and the
    `forward.installed` event carries the declared port as `declared_port`.
    [vmlab status](cmd_status) marks every shifted forward, however it was
    installed, and [vmlab port](cmd_port) prints the bound port for a script.
  DOC

  h2 "Editing the lab file between runs"

  p <<DOC
    `up` always runs the lab file as it is on disk now. The lab daemon reads
    `vmlab.wcl` when it starts, and again when a verb asks it to reload. A full [vmlab down](cmd_down) reaps it,
    so `down`, an edit and `up` start a fresh daemon that reads the edit. A
    daemon also outlives a partial `down`, a `vm stop` of every machine and an
    `up` that failed, and such a daemon may hold an older version of the
    file. Before it starts anything, `up` compares what the file declares now
    with what a running daemon loaded. If they differ and none of the lab's
    machines is running, `up` replaces the daemon with one that reads the
    file and prints one line:
  DOC
  code text { source = "lab \"ad-lab\": vmlab.wcl changed — restarting the lab daemon to load it" }
  p <<DOC
    Only what the file declares counts: an edit to a comment or to blank
    lines does not replace the daemon. Replacing the daemon keeps everything
    on disk: clones, snapshots, `.vmlab/state.json` (generated MACs and image
    pins) and the workspace ledgers. It is the same replacement
    [vmlab lab restart](cmd_lab) makes. [vmlab pull](cmd_pull),
    `vmlab vm start`, `vmlab vm restart` and their [container](cmd_container)
    spellings check the file the same way, because each builds a machine from
    the daemon's configuration.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    A new daemon cannot take over a machine the old one is running, nor a
    download the old one is doing. When the file has changed and any machine
    in the lab is running, starting, stopping or suspended, or a download is
    in flight, `up` asks the running daemon to reload the file instead. The
    daemon reads the file again, compares it machine by machine with what it
    holds, and applies what it can:
  DOC
  table {
    rows:
      | "Change" | "What the reload does" |
      | "A machine that is not running changed" | "Rebuilt from the new declaration: hardware, `qemu_args`, provisions, `depends_on`, `agent_update` and every other machine field. Its disks, MACs and `state.json` record stay." |
      | "A running machine changed" | "Keeps running on the declaration it was started with, and is **pending**. Its next boot rebuilds it first: `vmlab vm restart <m>`, or a stop and a start." |
      | "A machine added" | "Built, and joins the lab. It starts when asked, like any stopped machine." |
      | "A machine removed" | "Dropped if it is not running, pending until it stops if it is. Its disks stay until [vmlab destroy](cmd_destroy)." |
      | "A segment `forward {}` or a container `port {}`" | "Reinstalled at once for a running machine; a stopped one installs them when it boots." |
      | "The `on {}` handlers" | "Swapped at once." |
      | "A download in flight for a changed machine" | "That machine is pending; its next boot after the download rebuilds it." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    `up` prints one line per machine rebuilt, added, dropped or left pending,
    then carries on:
  DOC
  code text {
    source = <<TXT
lab "ad-lab": vmlab.wcl changed — reloading it into the running lab
srv01: rebuilt from the new declaration
dc01: pending — still running the declaration it was started with; restart it (`vmlab vm restart dc01`, or a stop and a start) to pick up the change
TXT
  }
  p <<DOC
    A pending machine is never quietly running the new file: `up`,
    `vm start` and [vmlab status](cmd_status) name it until it has booted on
    the new declaration.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    The network is assembled once, when the daemon starts, so a reload cannot
    change it. Adding, removing or changing a segment (its subnet, NAT, DHCP,
    DNS, MTU, routes, `global` or `connect`), the lab's DNS records and
    sinkholes, and a NIC's segment, static IP, MAC or `gateway` flag are
    network changes. So is an added machine whose NIC needs the assembled
    network to know it: a static IP, a `gateway` NIC, a global segment, or a
    segment the daemon does not have. On a network change the reload applies
    nothing, and `up` refuses, naming each change:
  DOC
  code text { source = "vmlab.wcl changes the lab's network, which cannot change while the lab runs: segment \"lan\" changed (mtu). Nothing was reloaded. Run `vmlab down`, then this command again, to apply the edit (or undo it)" }
  p <<DOC
    Shares served over SMB are the other lab-wide piece. The lab's SMB server
    exports a fixed set of shares. When a reload changes that set while a
    machine runs, the server keeps serving the old set and the reload says
    so. With nothing running it is stopped, and the next boot starts it on
    the new set. Shares that ride virtiofs follow their machine.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    Most edits simply take effect on the next boot of the machine. One kind
    cannot: a VM's clone is an overlay on the template it was made from, so it
    cannot move to a different `template`. vmlab records the template
    reference a clone was made from in `.vmlab/vms/<vm>/clone-source`. When
    the lab file names a different template while that clone still exists,
    `up` refuses before downloading or starting anything and keeps the clone:
  DOC
  code text { source = "vm \"web\": its disk is a clone of \"x86_64/alpine-3.23\", but vmlab.wcl now declares \"x86_64/debian-13\" — a clone cannot move to another template. Run `vmlab vm destroy web` (or `vmlab destroy`) to discard the disk and recreate it from \"x86_64/debian-13\", or put the template line back" }
  p <<DOC
    The reference is compared as written. An unpinned `<arch>/<name>` keeps
    its clone when a newer version of the same template is built, while
    pinning a different `@<version>` counts as a different template. A
    container's `image` line is different: changing it drops the image pin,
    and the next `up` pulls the new image (see
    [Containers](guide_containers)).
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    A lab daemon also decides, when it starts, whether the lab runs under
    [worktree isolation](guide_worktrees) and, in a worktree lab, which
    machines clone from the main checkout's freeze. When the main checkout
    has been frozen, frozen again or thawed since, or a machine has lost its
    disk, `up`, `pull`, `vm start` and `vm restart` replace the daemon the
    same way, in the main lab and in a worktree lab, and print the line
    below. A reload cannot take this change, since it decides how the network
    is assembled: while a machine is running or a download is in flight, the
    verb refuses, names what is in the way and points at `vmlab down`.
  DOC
  code text { source = "lab \"app-feat-x\": the main checkout's freeze or a machine's disk changed — restarting the lab daemon to plan its clones again" }

  h2 "Agent refresh"

  p <<DOC
    A VM's guest agent comes from its [template](guide_templates), so a
    template built by an older vmlab carries an older agent. When a VM's agent
    first answers, `up` compares the agent's version stamp (`agent=<rev>`)
    with the stamp of the agent this vmlab ships. If they differ, in either
    direction, `up` pushes the shipped agent into the VM the way
    [vmlab machine repair-agent](cmd_machine) does, and prints one line:
  DOC
  code text { source = "agent: updated \"winsrv\" (agent=57fa802 → agent=2284722)" }
  p <<DOC
    The VM is then **diverged**: its template's sealed `agent_version` no
    longer describes it, and [vmlab status](cmd_status) `-v` shows
    `diverged=yes` until its disks are destroyed. The refresh also emits
    `machine.agent_updated` (see the [event list](ref_events)). The refresh
    runs after the first-boot script, because that script belongs to the
    template and was written for the agent the template sealed, and before
    every provision script, so provisions see the current agent.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    `up` never starts waiting on a VM just to refresh its agent. A VM with no
    first-boot script, no provision or playbook and nothing depending on it is
    refreshed in the background when its agent first answers, after `up` has
    returned. Its line goes to the lab daemon's log instead of your terminal;
    the event and `diverged=yes` report it as usual. `vmlab vm start` always
    refreshes this way. A guest that never answers is never waited for.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    A VM being refreshed, inline by `up` or in the background, is not
    reported ready until its refresh has finished, whether it succeeded or
    failed, because the refresh restarts the agent. Until
    then `vmlab status` shows it `booting`, and anything that waits for
    readiness, such as a script's `wait_ready` or a dependent machine, keeps
    waiting. `vm.ready` arrives, and port forwards are installed, once the
    refresh is done. A VM whose agent is already current is held only for
    the comparison. A VM that cannot be refreshed is not held at all. The
    hold is capped at five minutes, after which the VM is reported ready and
    the refresh finishes in the background.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    Nothing else reaches the agent while it is being replaced. `up` returns
    without waiting on a VM nothing waits on, so a `vmlab exec` issued
    straight after it can arrive while the background refresh is still
    running. From before the push sends the binary until the new agent has
    answered a handshake as a new instance, every other use of that VM's
    agent, such as `exec`, `shell`, `cp` or a script, waits, for up to four
    minutes, and then runs on the new agent. A command already running on
    the channel when the push begins is allowed up to a minute to finish
    before the agent restarts. Without this, the first commands after `up`
    failed with `agent channel closed during exec` or exit code 137.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    On Windows the service restart lands a few seconds after the push asks
    for it, so the agent that answers the push's first reconnect is often
    still the old one. The push keeps waiting, within its two-minute budget,
    until that agent stops answering or a new instance answers, and only
    reports `the service did not restart onto it` when the budget is spent.
    Before this, a Server 2025 refresh was reported failed while its restart
    was still pending, and the restart then landed under the first command
    after `up`.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    A refresh never fails `up`. If it fails, `up` prints a `warning:` line
    that names the VM and the reason, emits `machine.agent_updated` with the
    error, and continues. The new binary is staged beside the installed one,
    so a push that fails leaves the old agent running. A VM stopped while its
    refresh still waits for the agent is not a failure: nothing was pushed, no
    warning is printed, and the event carries `stopped = true`. Containers are never
    refreshed, because their agent ships with this vmlab. Guests on the legacy
    agent tier are never refreshed either, because nothing can replace that
    agent over its own channel. A VM whose template sealed no agent is
    skipped too.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    Set `agent_update = false` on a `vm` block to keep the agent its template
    sealed. Set it on the `lab` block to do the same for every VM; a VM's own
    setting wins. See [vm and its children](ref_vm).
  DOC

  h2 "Recording a boot"

  p <<DOC
    `--record <vm>=<path>` records a VM's console from the first frame of
    its boot: QEMU starts paused, the recorder attaches, and only then does
    the VM continue. Every `--record` is checked before anything boots, and
    one refusal (a container, a bad size, no `ffmpeg`) arms none. The
    recording belongs to the lab daemon and continues after `up` returns,
    which prints `recording <vm> to <path>` after its last line; stop it
    with `vmlab record stop`, or let `vm stop`, `down` or `destroy`
    finalise it. A `--record` naming a VM that was already running records
    it from now. See [Console recording](guide_recording).
  DOC

  callout "A failed first-boot leaves the machine running" {
    kind = :note
    body = "A first-boot provision that errors, or that takes longer than 30 minutes, fails `up` but does not stop the machine, so you can open a console or a shell and look at what went wrong."
  }

  h2 "Examples"

  p "Bring up the whole lab in the current directory:"
  code sh { source = "vmlab up" }

  p "Bring up one machine and whatever it depends on:"
  code sh { source = "vmlab up client01" }

  p "Run the ad-lab example end to end:"
  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
cd examples/ad-lab
vmlab up
vmlab status
SRC
  }

  h2 "Exit status"

  p <<DOC
    Exit status is 0 when every target machine started and every provision
    step completed. A lab file that fails validation, a lab directory that
    cannot be found, a supervisor that does not come up, a boot or provision
    failure, an edit that cannot be applied while machines are running, or a
    VM whose template changed under its clone all exit 1, the `failed` code
    from the
    [error table](ref_protocol). Exit 5 (`conflict`) means the supervisor
    already tracks a lab with this name from a different directory; stop that
    lab or rename this one. A usage error the argument parser rejects exits 2.
    A share that will not mount is a warning, not a failure: the machines are
    up and the rest of the lab works, so `up` still exits 0.
  DOC
}
